0) One needs to to prove every assertion that one makes. Speculations will =
not be entertained unless they are specifically requested. Some items on ex=
ams may ask one to state a conjecture.

(1) In view of the above, one needs no feedback whatsoever to understand wh=
ether one's solution is correct or not. I regard the practice of listing an=
swers to problems as particularly harmful. Let me illustrate the point I wi=
sh to make using the following simple example that is part of the prerequis=
ites.

(2) For instance, if one solves the linear equation 2x+ 3 =3D5 by some algo=
rithm to get x =3D1, one can prove that one's solution is correct by substi=
tution as follows:

    LS <x <-- 1>
=3D (2x+3) <x <-- 1>
=3D 2(1) + 3

=3D 2 +3
=3D 5
=3D 5<x <-- 1>
=3D RS<x <-- 1>

This sequence of steps will lead to a contradiction if one's use of the alg=
orithm had yielded x =3D 2 as shown below:

 LS <x <-- 2>
=3D (2x+3)<x <-- 2>
=3D 2(2) + 3

=3D 4 +3
=3D 7

not equal to 5
=3D 5<x <-- 2>
=3D RS<x <-- 2>

This proves that the solution obtained is incorrect. In other words, there =
was some mistake in the application of the algorithm.

(3) No feedback from me regarding solutions is, therefore, necessary.

(4) The subject of mathematics rests solidly on the idea of PROOF. This ide=
as of feedback etc. arises from the pernicious practice of listing answers =
to problems. One who is making a claim IS REQUIRED to provide a PROOF.

(5) The syntax in which you are being required to present the work amounts =
to a very low level 'programming language'. If one writes a program which c=
ontains an error of syntax, the program does not run.  In the case at hand,=
 the error message is 0. That is the ONLY feedback a computer gives. The pr=
ogrammer must painstakingly go through the work to locate the error. This i=
s what is expected of every student.

(6) I shall strive my best to be as nearly like a computer as possible. If =
I deviate from this practice, please understand this to be a failure on my =
part and please let me know. I shall try to rectify myself immediately.

(9) This is a preliminary attempt on my part to put matters in perspective.

