rd the practice of listing answers to problems as particularly harmful. Let=
 me illustrate the point I wish to make
 using the following simple example that is part of the prerequisites.</str=
ong></p>
<p>(2) For instance, if one solves the linear equation 2x+ 3 =3D5 by some a=
lgorithm to get x =3D1, one can prove that one's solution is correct by sub=
stitution as follows:</p>
<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; LS &lt;x &lt;-- 1&gt;&nbsp;<br>
=3D (2x+3) &lt;x &lt;-- 1&gt;&nbsp;<br>
=3D 2(1) + 3</p>
<p>=3D 2 +3&nbsp;<br>
=3D 5&nbsp;<br>
=3D 5&lt;x &lt;-- 1&gt;&nbsp;<br>
=3D RS&lt;x &lt;-- 1&gt;</p>
<p>This sequence of steps will lead to a contradiction if one's use of the =
algorithm had yielded x =3D 2 as shown below:</p>
<p>&nbsp;LS &lt;x &lt;-- 2&gt;&nbsp;<br>
=3D (2x+3)&lt;x &lt;-- 2&gt;&nbsp;<br>
=3D 2(2) + 3</p>
<p>=3D 4 +3&nbsp;<br>
=3D 7</p>
<p>not equal to 5&nbsp;<br>
=3D 5&lt;x &lt;-- 2&gt;&nbsp;<br>
=3D RS&lt;x &lt;-- 2&gt;</p>
<p>This proves that the solution obtained is incorrect. In other words, the=
re was some mistake in the application of the algorithm.</p>
<p>(3) No feedback from me regarding solutions is, therefore, necessary.</p=
>
<p>(4)<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>The subject of mathematics rests solidly o=
n the idea of PROOF. This ideas of feedback etc. arises from the pernicious=
 practice of listing answers to problems. One who is making a claim IS REQU=
IRED to provide a PROOF.</strong></p>
<p><span>(5)</span><span>&nbsp;</span><strong>The syntax in which you are b=
eing required to present the work amounts to a very low level 'programming =
language'. If one writes a program which contains an error of syntax, the p=
rogram does not run. &nbsp;In the case at
 hand, the error message is 0. That is the ONLY feedback a computer gives. =
The programmer must painstakingly go through the work to locate the error. =
This is what is expected of every student</strong><span>.</span></p>
<p>(6)<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>I shall strive my best to be as nearly lik=
e a computer as possible. If I deviate from this practice, please understan=
d this to be a failure on my part and please let me know. I shall try to re=
ctify myself immediately</strong>. &nbsp;</p>
